Done. The fix is one line in `lib/request.js`:

```js
port: {
  get () {
    const portReg = /(?<port>:\d+)$/
    // `host` is the effective host: with `trustProxy` enabled it may come
    // from `x-forwarded-host`, so `host`, `hostname` and `port` stay aligned.
    const matches = portReg.exec(this.host)
    ...
```

**What changed** (`lib/request.js:259`): the `port` getter now parses `this.host` instead of re-reading `this.headers.host ?? this.headers[':authority']`. The parsing logic from #6603 (regex, `null` when absent, IPv6-safe) is untouched. Because `port` lives on the base `Request.prototype` and the trust-proxy variant overrides only `host`, `this.host` resolves through the overridden getter — so `host`, `hostname`, and `port` all derive from the same effective host, including the `x-forwarded-host` and `:authority` paths. The two-variant prototype construction (`buildRegularRequest` / `buildRequestWithTrustProxy`, `kHasBeenDecorated`) is preserved; no new getter was added to the trust-proxy variant.

**Co-change partner** (`lib/reply.js`, 40% co-change): opened it — it has no references to `port`, `host`, or `hostname`; it reads request state only via `kRouteContext`/`kRequestSignal`/`kTimeoutTimer`. It did **not** need a change.

**Tests** (`test/request-port.test.js`): added a trust-proxy case table built with `Request.buildRequest(Request, true)`, asserting `{host, hostname, port}` together so drift can't pass again — `x-forwarded-host` winning over `host`, last entry of a multi-value `x-forwarded-host`, forwarded host without a port, IPv6 forwarded host, `host` fallback, `:authority` fallback, and no host at all. Both tests in the file pass. Under the old code the first case would have returned `3000` (from `host`) while `request.host` said `example.com:8080`.

Per your one-file limit I ran only `test/request-port.test.js`, so the diagnostics-channel and reply/stream suites that COMPLEX.md recommends for `lib/request.js` edits were not run. Nothing committed.

